For most of modern history, one number told the energy story, the price of a barrel of crude. This year, the price of crude no longer reflects what consumers actually pay. Crude oil cannot simply be used as fuel; it must first be refined into diesel. However, the world’s capacity to produce diesel has been severely impacted.
The war involving Iran has rewritten the market's structure. The attack began in February, and Iran's closure of the Strait of Hormuz for much of the year has pushed up prices and unsettled markets, including diesel. The International Energy Agency said the Gulf's net exports of diesel and gasoil in August stood at just over a quarter of their pre-war level. Thomas Kloza of Gulf Oil says the world has lost about 7 million barrels a day since early 2026, which is why American refineries now run at nearly 97 percent of capacity. US distillate inventories sit near three-decade seasonal lows. The system has almost no cushion left for the next shock.
The crack spread, the gap between diesel and the crude used to make it, has widened further. Bloomberg reported that the US diesel crack rose above $106 per barrel on September 1, an all-time record, after breaching $100 for the first time in August.
Stillwater Associates notes that crude has moved up and down this year, and diesel cracks set fresh records on both moves. US diesel sits at a record $6.45 a gallon this week, up 74 percent from a year ago.
Part of the reason for the widening spread is that refineries have also become targets. Vortexa counted 32 attacks on Russian refineries in July and August, and one strike left the Perm plant with only about 28 percent of its crude distillation capacity working. Russia's seaborne diesel exports averaged only about 1,50,000 barrels a day in the first 25 days of August, roughly 6,10,000 barrels a day below a year earlier. Moscow has banned diesel exports until October.
Trump's intervention shows how much the target list has changed. In earlier wars, the fear centred mostly on crude fields, pipelines and export terminals. This time, the American president had to make it clear that refineries are the sensitive target.
Speaking in Ireland on September 13, he told Zelenskyy there were plenty of other targets and that Kyiv should leave diesel alone because the attacks were hurting the world. Trump then announced that both sides had agreed to stop, but Reuters reports that the attacks on energy infrastructure continued.
The proof came on Sunday. Moscow Mayor Sergey Sobyanin said 450 Ukrainian drones were shot down on their way to the capital, and several reached the Moscow Oil Refinery. Reuters called it the largest Ukrainian drone attack on the Moscow region. The plant processed 11.6 million tonnes of oil in 2024, including 3.2 million tonnes of diesel. The strike came just days after Trump's plea, and it shows how little control Washington has over a market that now hinges on a few hundred refineries.
India stands better protected than most. It has installed refining capacity of 258.1 million tonnes a year, which exceeds domestic fuel demand, and it exported 61.5 million tonnes of petroleum products in 2025-26. Indian refineries supplied about 60 percent of the diesel passing through the Bab el-Mandeb towards Europe in August, roughly 2,00,000 barrels a day in all, according to Vortexa. An economy that had to buy finished fuel at today's margins would face far heavier inflation and a much larger import bill, and India's spare capacity has spared it that. 
