Indian equity benchmarks extended their slide for a sixth straight week in the week ended September 18, as volatile crude oil prices and mixed global cues weighed on sentiment. The NIFTY 50 slipped 51.70 points, or 0.2 percent. The NIFTY Midcap 100 settled flat and the NIFTY Smallcap 100 fell 0.2 percent.
Oil prices swung through the week. Brent crude touched a high of $108.75 per barrel before retreating to close at $103.87 on Friday. Reports that Saudi Arabia was offering extra crude cargoes to Asian refiners helped ease supply worries. Crude oil moved sharply higher at the start of the week after attacks on pipeline infrastructure in Saudi Arabia.
Investors also remained cautious as the US 10-year bond yield rose to 5.03 per cent, its highest level since 2007, ahead of the Federal Reserve’s rate decision. On Wednesday, the Fed raised its benchmark rate by 25 basis points to a range of 3.75 percent to 4 percent after a two-day policy meeting. It was the first hike in more than three years.
Sectorally speaking, the NIFTY India Defence index fell 3.8 percent and the NIFTY Consumer Durables index lost 2.7 percent, while NIFTY Auto index slipped 0.6 percent. On the other hand, NIFTY Media gained 1.1 percent while NIFTY FMCG rose 0.9 percent. A positive signal was the 7.3 percent drop in India VIX.
The major US stock indexes finished mixed in a week shaped by the Fed’s rate hike and swings in oil prices driven by the Middle East conflict. The Fed lifted the federal funds target rate by 25 basis points to 3.75 percent to 4 percent, as markets widely expected. The FOMC’s summary of economic projections shows policymakers expect one more 25 basis point hike by the end of 2026.
Indian markets are likely to take cues from the oil market. Any fresh turn in the West Asia conflict, or further clarity on how much Saudi supply can reach Asian buyers, could swing Brent again after its move between $108.75 and $103.87 last week. US bond yields can also impact global equity markets.
On the weekly chart, the Nifty closed at 23,346.40, down 0.22 percent for the week. The index has broken down from the rising wedge pattern and is now nearing the lower trendline, which has historically served as strong support. Prices continue to trade below the 40-week EMA, a sign of some bearishness in the broader trend. The RMI indicator has also shown a bearish crossover, adding weight to the negative bias and pointing to continued weakness in the near term.
The FII Net Index Futures Position has hit a new extreme low of 2,88,428, an all-time low zone for FII selling positions. This suggests that FIIs are extremely bearish on the market. Such extreme positioning can also work as a contrarian signal, though. If the market starts to recover, FIIs could cover their short positions, which could support a broader market recovery.
Another momentum indicator, the Weekly Swing, is also in oversold territory. On the weekly timeframe, the market has historically seen a sharp recovery or bounce whenever this indicator has reached the oversold zone. A similar setup is visible now, with the indicator at 22.68 and showing signs of strength as it turns up from oversold levels. This suggests that a recovery or bounce in the market can be expected going forward.
Another derivatives-based indicator is the Call-Minus-Put OI Histogram. Whenever the indicator moves above the 5-lakh level, it has historically acted as a buy signal during market declines. A similar setup was observed a few days ago, when the indicator moved above 5-lakh. Similar signals were also seen in May and June, followed by positive market reactions. This setup therefore indicates the possibility of a positive reaction going forward.
